  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 140 PM MDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nThe ridge will become much more established over AZ on Thursday and \nFriday, allowing the drier air to spread south-southeastward across \nmuch of the northern and western portions of NM. This will produce a \nstark moisture gradient across the state. With the combination of \nincreasing stability, weakening low-level convergence, and drier mid-\nlevels will cause quite the decrease in precipitation coverage. Any \nshowers and storms that do manage to develop will likely favor the \nGila and Sacramento Mountains. Temperatures will also respond to the \nsettling of the ridge, trending upwards (c'mon now it's\nSeptember--we should be trending downwards!), with portions of the\nCWA potentially reaching 5-15 degrees above normal by late in the\nweek. \n\nThings become a bit more favorable for the return of moisture and \nprecipitation over the weekend as the ridge shifts back east and a \ntrough digs south across the Rockies. This setup will allow for a \nmore west-southwesterly flow across the state, bringing in moisture \nfrom the Gulf of California. This moisture return should be \nsufficient enough to support renewed precipitation chances, \nmainly across the west and areas of higher terrain. By early next \nweek, the trough shifts farther east while the minimal moisture \nplume remains in place. This should allow at least scattered rain\nchances to persist, with temperatures progged to gradually ease \ncloser to seasonal averages, particularly across western NM.",
